00:25:28.716 --> 00:25:34.916
But how do you scale? Right. So after creating a custom
transformation and applying it to one particular project,

00:25:34.916 --> 00:25:39.556
how then do you apply that transformation
to multiple thousands of different projects?

00:25:39.556 --> 00:25:45.516
Now, since AWS Transform custom is a command line interface based,
you can wrap it up in a batch script,

00:25:45.516 --> 00:25:51.396
and all you need is a shell to run transformations
in batch mode and scale it across your enterprise.

00:25:51.396 --> 00:25:52.876
So let's see this in action.

00:25:54.396 --> 00:25:58.196
Over here I have a batch script that's called Batch Launcher.

00:25:58.196 --> 00:26:03.236
It's a wrapper around AWS Transform
custom to execute transformations in parallel.

00:26:04.236 --> 00:26:10.276
So if you take a look at the script, it takes
in a CSV file as input that contains information about my repository.

00:26:10.676 --> 00:26:18.636
And I've also configured options such as the number of maximum
parallel jobs, the output directory, the build commands,

00:26:18.676 --> 00:26:25.356
so on and so forth. And the next thing I'm going to do is I'll open up
my CSV file so that you can take a look

00:26:25.396 --> 00:26:32.756
and you'll see that it consists a bunch of GitHub repositories
and things like the repository path build command transformation,

00:26:32.756 --> 00:26:36.356
name validation commands, as well as additional plan contexts.

00:26:37.076 --> 00:26:45.036
I'll then run open up my run Gradle upgrade script that will run
my batch launcher script, and it's taking in my CSV file as input,

00:26:45.356 --> 00:26:49.876
I will specify a maximum number of ten
transformation jobs to run in parallel.

00:26:50.516 --> 00:26:53.596
And what I'll do next is I'll run that Gradle upgrade script.

00:26:53.836 --> 00:27:00.916
And what happens is that there will be ten transformation jobs
running at the same time all through AWS Transform Customer AI.

00:27:00.916 --> 00:27:03.956
It will then generate a summary of all the transformations.

00:27:04.196 --> 00:27:10.156
And similar to the first demo, it's actually using out
of the box transformation definition that AWS provided.

00:27:10.156 --> 00:27:14.556
So if you look over here, you will see the summary
as well as the detailed results.

00:27:17.156 --> 00:27:22.996
All right. So now we've seen how you can perform out of the box
transformations, create your own custom transformations.

00:27:22.996 --> 00:27:28.596
And we have also seen how you can scale these transformations
all through AWS Transform custom.

00:27:29.596 --> 00:27:31.236
Now let's talk about the last part.

00:27:31.236 --> 00:27:36.276
Continual learning. How does
AWS Transform custom agent become smarter over time?

00:27:36.636 --> 00:27:42.236
So for this particular demo, I'll show you how you
can capture knowledge items, list them, review them,

00:27:42.676 --> 00:27:48.396
and enable them for future runs so that your AWS Transform
custom agent can become smarter over time.

00:27:51.036 --> 00:27:55.916
All right, so for this demo, I'll be using
the same demo environment as the previous ones.

00:27:56.196 --> 00:28:03.636
And what I'll do is I'll run ATX dev exec to execute
the Java version upgrade for multiple different projects.

00:28:05.236 --> 00:28:08.956
And now you can also assume that all
these transformations have been completed.

00:28:08.956 --> 00:28:12.356
And the next thing I'll do is I'll run ATX custom dev list key.

00:28:12.716 --> 00:28:19.396
So key stands for Knowledge Items. To list all of the knowledge
items that were captured during agent execution.

00:28:19.396 --> 00:28:25.596
So some of these knowledge items could actually improve
your future code transformations across your organizations.

00:28:25.596 --> 00:28:31.196
So if you look over here, for example,
SolarWinds API should use a specific query path.

00:28:31.236 --> 00:28:35.156
Gradle shadow plugin outputs should output
the artifacts to a specific path.

00:28:35.156 --> 00:28:42.236
So on and so forth. And what I'm going to do next is I'm going
to pick up some of this generic knowledge items and I'll enable it.

00:28:42.236 --> 00:28:46.516
Right? So what this means is that the next time I run
AWS Transform custom agent,

00:28:46.756 --> 00:28:50.676
this knowledge items will become part
of the transformations knowledge base.

00:28:50.676 --> 00:28:57.796
And this makes your future transformations more accurate
and aligned to your organization's specific rules and standards.

00:29:01.116 --> 00:29:06.396
All right, so to wrap this up, let me just
highlight a key features of AWS Transform custom.

00:29:06.796 --> 00:29:09.476
So firstly, continual learning capability.

00:29:09.716 --> 00:29:15.116
AWS Transform custom automatically captures
and applies both explicit developer feedback

00:29:15.276 --> 00:29:19.356
and implicit feedback through agent
execution to become smarter over time.

00:29:20.036 --> 00:29:23.356
Secondly, guided transformation definition creation.

00:29:23.516 --> 00:29:26.996
When you're creating your transformation
definition for the first time,

00:29:26.996 --> 00:29:31.876
AWS Transform custom uses built
in feedback loops to detect missing contexts.

00:29:32.036 --> 00:29:38.716
If there's any ambiguity, it proactively prompts you for additional
instructions so that your definitions are complete.

00:29:39.436 --> 00:29:42.836
Thirdly, the concept of learn once transform everywhere.

00:29:43.396 --> 00:29:48.596
Your developers shouldn't be spending duplicative
efforts building the same transformation definitions over

00:29:48.596 --> 00:29:51.636
and over again with AWS Transform customers.

00:29:51.756 --> 00:29:57.436
You create your transformation definition once vetted once,
and publish it to your internal registry,

00:29:57.596 --> 00:30:00.000
making it available across your entire organization instantly.

